faq:
  - question: How much should a SaaS company spend on SEO?
    answer: >-
      Between $3,000 and $8,000 a month at seed to Series A, $8,000 to
      $15,000 at growth stage, and $15,000 or more for enterprise programmes.
      Below $3,000 a month you are funding a single channel rather than a
      complete programme, which is usually a false economy.
  - question: Is a SaaS-specialist agency better than a generalist?
    answer: >-
      For most SaaS companies, yes. Generalists tend to target high-volume
      keywords with no commercial intent. SaaS sites also have specific
      technical problems that generalists often miss: JavaScript rendering,
      indexation gaps on product pages, login walls hiding pricing pages, and
      cannibalisation across blog and docs.
  - question: Which of these agencies publish their pricing?
    answer: >-
      Embarque publishes a flat fee from $1,500 a month. Powered by Search
      publishes a full rate card, with SEO consulting from $9,000 a month.
      Skale and SimpleTiger publish nothing on their own sites, so their
      minimums come from Clutch. The rest quote custom or band pricing, which
      is why we show ranges.
  - question: What makes SaaS SEO different from regular SEO?
    answer: >-
      SaaS buyers compare products, read use-case pages and sign up for
      trials. So the work centres on comparison and alternative pages,
      bottom-funnel keywords, product-led content and the technical issues of
      app-based sites. Traffic volume matters less than signups and pipeline.
  - question: How long before SEO produces pipeline?
    answer: >-
      Expect six to twelve months for measurable traffic and lead increases,
      and twelve to eighteen for full return. Technical fixes can show
      movement in weeks; revenue impact does not.
  - question: What is GEO, and does it matter yet?
    answer: >-
      [Generative engine optimisation](/best-geo-agencies/) is optimising to
      appear in AI-generated answers from ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini and
      Google's AI Overviews. It matters now and is becoming a standard part
      of SaaS SEO retainers. If an agency has no answer on it, that tells you
      how current their thinking is.
  - question: Should I hire an agency or build in-house?
    answer: >-
      For most Series A and Series B companies, an agency delivers better
      output per dollar in the first two years. Once SEO becomes a core
      growth channel, a hybrid model, agency plus an in-house lead, tends to
      make more sense than either alone.
  - question: Can I trust agency case studies?
    answer: >-
      Treat them as marketing until verified. Ask for the client's name, the
      timeframe, the starting baseline, and a live URL you can check
      yourself. Percentage growth figures without a baseline are close to
      meaningless: 2,500% growth from a hundred visitors is a hundred
      visitors.
---
## How we chose these nine

We started from the buyer, not the agencies. A seed-stage founder and a Series C marketing lead need different things, so every entry says who it fits. We then built the pool by reading which agencies independent sources name repeatedly, not which agencies name themselves. Pricing comes from each agency's own site or its Clutch profile; where we could not confirm a figure we wrote "Custom". Every entry gets real cons, and we do not remove a con because an agency asks. Full method: [How we rank](/how-we-rank/).

## Agencies we looked at and left out

Onely, First Page Sage, TripleDart and DerivateX came up repeatedly in our research, mostly in lists they publish themselves. They are not on this list for four different reasons. Onely serves eBay, IKEA, Philip Morris and Al Jazeera rather than SaaS exclusively. First Page Sage runs dedicated HVAC, law firm and private equity practices. TripleDart is a full-stack GTM agency for B2B tech and D2C rather than an SEO specialist. DerivateX is a two-founder agency that ranks itself first in all three roundups it publishes. Typical reasons: no SaaS-only focus, no independent mentions we could verify, or pricing we could not confirm. If that changes, we will add them.

## Why most "best SaaS SEO agency" lists can't be trusted

Search "best SaaS SEO agencies" and nearly every result is published by an SEO agency. Onely's guide ranks Onely first, and to their credit they disclose it openly. Powered by Search, First Page Sage, Embarque and DerivateX all appear in lists they publish themselves. TripleDart's ranking lives on TripleDart's website.

This is not necessarily dishonest, and some of those lists are useful. But it does mean the "consensus" you are reading is largely agencies citing each other, and the top slot is often occupied by whoever owns the domain.

We do not sell SEO services. This list is built on which agencies are named repeatedly by sources with no stake in the answer, then segmented by what each one is best at.

### The problem is worse than self-promotion

In May 2026, Alex Birkett, co-founder of Omniscient Digital and one of the two most-recommended agencies in this category, published a review of his own company. He explained why. Agencies he had never heard of were publishing dozens of comparison pages about Omniscient Digital. Some contained what he described as deliberate factual inaccuracies about their services, pricing and capabilities.

He called it "negative AEO": an attempt to denigrate a competitor's brand and siphon its credibility inside AI search results. Rather than chase corrections across other people's domains, he wrote the accurate version on his own.

That episode tells you two useful things. First, some of the comparison content ranking for these terms is adversarial rather than merely lazy. Second, when an agency's own founder has to publish a correction, no list can be taken on trust. The fastest way to check any claim here, including ours, is to open the agency's own site and verify it.

## What SaaS SEO actually costs in 2026

Most lists omit pricing entirely, which makes them close to useless for shortlisting. Here are the real bands.

**$3,000 to $8,000 a month** buys foundational work: keyword strategy, roughly four to ten content pieces a month, basic technical SEO and light link building. This is the right band for pre-seed through Series A. At this budget the right expectation is to validate whether organic search is a viable channel for you, not to scale it.

**$8,000 to $15,000 a month** is where most growth-stage SaaS lands. At this level you get full content strategy and production of roughly fifteen to twenty-five pieces monthly, dedicated link building or digital PR, conversion optimisation, and increasingly generative engine optimisation for AI search.

**$15,000 a month and up** covers enterprise programmes with full-funnel demand generation, and can run past $30,000 for large-scale work.

Below $3,000 a month you are buying one channel, not a programme. Broader market data puts the average agency retainer at around $3,200 a month across all industries (Ahrefs, poll of 439 SEO service providers, December 2023), but SaaS sits above that. The work requires category positioning, comparison pages, use-case content and close coordination with product marketing.

## How to choose by stage

**Pre-seed to Series A.** Your question is not "which agency is best" but "is organic search viable for us at all". Pick a lean specialist, cap the budget, and set a six-month checkpoint with a specific success metric. Embarque and SimpleTiger's entry tier are built for this.

**Series B to C.** Now you are building an engine. This is where content quality compounds and where the premium agencies earn their fee. Omniscient Digital if quality is the priority, Grow and Convert if conversion is the gap, Skale if signup volume is the metric.

**Enterprise.** SEO stops being a channel and becomes one input into demand generation across a long sales cycle and a buying committee. Powered by Search and Directive operate at this level.

## The questions that actually separate agencies

Ask for SaaS case studies at your ARR stage, because a great result for a $50M company tells you nothing about your seed-stage site. Ask for pipeline metrics rather than traffic: demos, signups, qualified leads. Ask them to walk you through a currently-ranking article for a past client and explain how it converts. Ask how they approach AI search visibility, since buyers increasingly start in ChatGPT and Perplexity rather than Google. And insist on meeting the strategist who will own your account, not just the one who shows up to the pitch.

One warning sign worth taking seriously: an agency that treats your SaaS product like a local business or an ecommerce store. It will target keywords with no commercial intent, and you will get traffic that never converts.

## How long this takes

The first three months are foundational: audits, fixes, strategy. Early ranking movement typically shows around months four to six. Measurable traffic and lead increases usually land between months six and twelve, and full return often takes twelve to eighteen months. Any agency promising first-position rankings in thirty days is telling you something useful about themselves.
